<p>After a hailstorm, a burst pipe, or a kitchen fire, most people expect the insurance claim to be the easy part. It rarely is. Policies are long, the first estimate can miss damage, and the insurer has its own adjusters and reviewers working the file. San Marcos Public Adjuster was set up so that property owners in San Marcos and across Hays County have a licensed professional working for them in that process. As a San Marcos public adjuster, we represent the insured, never the insurance company.</p>
<p>This page explains who we are, how a public adjuster differs from the adjuster your insurer sends, the Texas rules we work under, and how we handle claims.</p>

<h2>Who operates San Marcos Public Adjuster</h2>
<p>San Marcos Public Adjuster is a public adjusting service of <strong>[[company]]</strong>, licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance (TDI license #[[license]]). We serve San Marcos, Hays County, and surrounding Central Texas communities. We do not run a storefront office; we meet clients at the property, where the damage can be seen and measured.</p>

<h2>Meet Joseph Dittman</h2>
<p>Claims are led by <a href="/author/joseph-dittman/">Joseph Dittman</a>, a licensed Texas public adjuster, certified insurance appraiser, insurance umpire, and expert witness. His experience covers residential, commercial, storm, hail, wind, water, fire, hurricane, and catastrophe losses. His background includes carrier-side adjusting, public adjusting, appraisal work, expert witness support, and hands-on construction.</p>
<p>Each part of that background is useful on a claim. Carrier-side work shows how insurers build and review estimates. Appraisal and umpire work shows how disagreements over the amount of a loss actually get resolved. Construction experience helps answer the practical question behind every estimate: what does it really take to put this building back? Joseph also writes and reviews the guides on our <a href="/blog/">insurance claim blog</a>.</p>
<p>You can reach Joseph directly at [[joseph-email]], or use the general inbox [[email]].</p>

<h2>Insurance company adjuster vs. public adjuster</h2>
<p>Most people first meet an adjuster after they report a loss. That adjuster, whether a staff employee or an independent adjuster hired for a storm, is working on behalf of the insurance company. A public adjuster is licensed to work for the policyholder on the same claim. The roles compare like this:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Insurer's adjuster</th><th>Public adjuster</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Whose interests do they represent?</td><td>The insurance company</td><td>You, the insured</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who pays them?</td><td>The insurer or a firm it hires</td><td>You, under a written contract with fees disclosed up front</td></tr>
<tr><td>What is the job?</td><td>Evaluate the claim under the insurer's guidelines</td><td>Document, value, and present the full covered loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>How is the policy read?</td><td>Through the insurer's interpretation</td><td>With an eye to every coverage that may apply to your loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>How many files at once?</td><td>Often a heavy caseload after a regional storm</td><td>Your property and your policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Texas licensing</td><td>Adjuster license</td><td>Public adjuster license under Insurance Code Chapter 4102</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For a deeper comparison, read <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster/">public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster</a>. If you are still deciding whether you need help at all, start with <a href="/do-i-need-a-public-adjuster/">do I need a public adjuster?</a></p>

<h2>Texas rules every public adjuster must follow</h2>
<p>Public adjusting is a licensed, regulated profession in Texas. The <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> oversees it under <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.4102.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102</a>. The rules that matter most to you:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A written contract comes first.</strong> Representation begins only with a contract on TDI-approved terms. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</li>
<li><strong>You can change your mind.</strong> You may rescind the contract within 72 hours of signing.</li>
<li><strong>"We represent the insured only."</strong> Texas public adjuster contracts must say so in plain terms.</li>
<li><strong>No repair conflicts.</strong> A public adjuster may not participate in repairing or rebuilding a property on a claim they adjust.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before you sign with anyone, check the license on TDI's website. We encourage it.</p>

<h2>How we handle a claim</h2>
<p>We do not use templates or one-size claim packages. A hail claim on a composition shingle roof in a newer Kyle subdivision and a flood loss in an older home near the San Marcos River raise very different questions. Our process usually includes:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Inspection</strong> of visible and hidden damage, from the roof and exterior to interiors, attics, and building systems.</li>
<li><strong>Documentation</strong> with photos, measurements, and notes, often supported by Xactimate estimating software and Matterport 3D imaging.</li>
<li><strong>Policy review</strong> of your declarations page, coverages, endorsements, deductibles (including any separate wind/hail deductible), and exclusions.</li>
<li><strong>Claim presentation</strong> with a detailed estimate and supporting file submitted to the insurer.</li>
<li><strong>Negotiation and follow-up</strong>, including <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplements</a> or the policy's <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> process when appropriate.</li>
</ol>
<p>You will know who is working on your claim, what has been sent to the insurer, and what comes next. Sometimes the honest answer is that you do not need us: the insurer's offer looks fair, or the damage is close to your deductible. When that is the case, we will say so.</p>

<h2>Our ethics</h2>
<p>A claim is only as strong as its credibility. We will never:</p>
<ul>
<li>Inflate damage or add items that are not part of the loss</li>
<li>Misstate facts to an insurer</li>
<li>Promise a settlement amount or outcome</li>
<li>Pressure anyone to sign a contract</li>
</ul>
<p>We will:</p>
<ul>
<li>Present legitimate losses completely and accurately</li>
<li>Answer unsupported denials with evidence and policy language</li>
<li>Expect the insurer to honor the policy you paid for</li>
<li>Tell you plainly when an issue needs a Texas attorney rather than a public adjuster</li>
</ul>
<p>We are not attorneys and not contractors. We do not give legal advice or file lawsuits, and we do not perform repairs. You stay in control of who fixes your property.</p>

